---
import SystemPage from '../../components/v2/SystemPage.astro';
import { imagery } from '../../content/imagery';

const v2 = imagery.v2;
---

<SystemPage
	title="Metal roofs · residential"
	heroEyebrow="Residential · materials"
	heroHeading="Metal roofs.<br />40 years on a house."
	heroLede="Standing seam and exposed-fastener metal for East Texas homes. Steel or aluminum. Class 4 impact, Class A fire, 30–40 year paint warranty. The roof you only buy once."
	heroPhoto={v2.pageHeros.metalRoofs}
	heroCta={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	bleedPhoto={v2.bleeds.crew}
	bleedHeading="Concealed fasteners.<br />No gaskets to fail."
	overview={{
		eyebrow: 'Why metal',
		heading: 'Buy once. Stay 40 years.',
		body: "Residential metal is the answer when you're staying in the house. A correctly-installed standing seam roof outlasts the house's other systems by decades — most owners replace the HVAC twice and the water heater three times before they touch the roof. The trade-off is up-front cost: metal runs roughly 2–3x the per-square cost of asphalt. Whether that pencils depends on how long you're staying."
	}}
	keyPoints={[
		{
			heading: 'Standing seam — the long-term answer.',
			body: "Concealed-fastener panels with no exposed screws or gaskets in the field of the roof. Steel substrate (Galvalume) with PVDF (Kynar 500) finish. McElroy Maxima/138T/238T, Drexel, Mueller, and Berridge are the primary product lines. 30–40 year paint warranties standard.",
			bullets: ['Concealed fasteners, no exposed gaskets', 'PVDF paint · 30–40 yr warranty', 'Class 4 impact · UL 2218', 'Class A fire · UL 790'],
			photo: v2.materialTiles.metal,
			photoSide: 'left',
		},
		{
			heading: 'Exposed-fastener (R-panel).',
			body: "R-panel, PBR, or Max-Rib panels with screws through the rib. Cheaper up-front, faster install, but the rubber gaskets under the screw heads degrade over time. Right for outbuildings, ag, detached shops. We install both — but on the main house, we usually steer to standing seam.",
			bullets: ['Cheaper up-front', 'Right for shops, outbuildings, ag', '15–25 yr window before re-fastening', 'Easier color match for repairs'],
			photo: v2.bleeds.workmanship,
			photoSide: 'right',
		},
	]}
	stats={[
		{ value: '40+', label: 'Years service life · standing seam' },
		{ value: '40 yr', label: 'PVDF paint warranty', numericValue: 40, suffix: ' yr' },
		{ value: 'Class 4', label: 'Impact rating · UL 2218' },
		{ value: '0', label: 'Exposed fasteners · standing seam' },
	]}
	projectPhotos={[v2.projects[2], v2.projects[3], v2.projects[7], v2.projects[8]]}
	projectCaptions={['Standing seam over architectural roof', 'Metal accent + asphalt main, Tyler', 'Full standing seam re-roof', 'Pre-painted Galvalume, Kilgore']}
	faq={[
		{ q: 'Standing seam or exposed-fastener?', a: "<p>For your house: standing seam, almost always. The longer warranty, no exposed gaskets, and cleaner look pay back the cost difference over the time you're going to be in the house. Exposed-fastener belongs on the shop, the barn, or a detached structure where the budget rules and the roof failure window is acceptable.</p>" },
		{ q: 'Will it dent in hail?', a: "<p>Class 4 impact rating means a 2-inch steel ball dropped from 20 feet without cracking the panel. Cosmetic denting is possible in significant hail (1.5\"+) but functional failure is rare. Most insurers offer hail-claim discounts on Class 4 metal.</p>" },
		{ q: 'Is metal louder when it rains?', a: '<p>No, when installed over a residential deck. The decking, underlayment, and attic insulation absorb impact noise — properly installed metal roofs are no louder inside the house than asphalt. The "metal roofs are loud" idea comes from un-decked metal on barns and pole buildings.</p>' },
		{ q: 'How does the cost compare to asphalt?', a: "<p>Roughly 2–3x per square. So an asphalt re-roof at $X is a metal re-roof at $2–3X. The math depends on how long you're staying — over 30 years, metal usually wins on total cost; over 10 years, asphalt does.</p>" },
		{ q: 'Will it strip the paint off my car? (lightning)', a: '<p>Metal roofs are no more lightning-prone than any other roof type — actually slightly less, because metal dissipates rather than concentrating energy. Insurance carriers don\'t penalize metal roofs for lightning risk.</p>' },
		{ q: 'Steel or aluminum?', a: "<p>Steel for nearly every East Texas home. Aluminum's advantage is corrosion resistance in salt-air environments — not a factor more than 50 miles inland. Steel is stronger, cheaper, and the standard from every major manufacturer.</p>" },
	]}
	ctaHeading="Free inspection.<br />Honest answer on metal."
	ctaLede="Same-day or next-day quote. We tell you whether metal makes sense for your house before we quote it."
	ctaPhoto={v2.pageHeros.freeInspection}
	ctaPrimary={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	promises={['Insurance accepted', '30–40 yr paint warranty', 'Locally owned + crewed']}
/>
